  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Isolate $H(s)$ from the Laplace-domain input-output relation by dividing $Y(s)$ by $X(s)$ and interpret the result as the system-only multiplier for an LTI system.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, the Laplace-domain input-output relation is $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. The object to recognize here is $H(s)$: it is the system-only factor, separate from the particular input and output transforms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $X(s)\\neq 0$, divide both sides by $X(s)$ to isolate the system factor. This is the allowed algebraic move that removes the input dependence and leaves only the transfer function.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transfer function is not a new signal; it is the ratio that describes the LTI system itself. In later work, the same $H(s)$ stays with the system while different inputs change $X(s)$ and $Y(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)\\neq 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The base case is simple cancellation: if $X(s)$ and $Y(s)$ share the same factor, divide it out and read the remaining constant or rational factor as $H(s)$. That is the core move this section prepares.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Isolating $H(s)$ from $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=\\frac{4}{s+1}$ and $Y(s)=\\frac{10}{s+1}$, find $H(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product relation $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. To isolate the system factor, divide both sides by $X(s)$, assuming $X(s)\\neq 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the given transforms. Because both $X(s)$ and $Y(s)$ share the factor $(s+1)$, the ratio should cancel that shared term cleanly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{\\frac{10}{s+1}}{\\frac{4}{s+1}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After cancellation, $H(s)=\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}$. This is the transfer function for the system in this example: the input factor is removed, and only the system multiplier remains.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding $H(s)$ from Given Input and Output Transforms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s-3}$ and $Y(s)=\\frac{8}{s-3}$, find $H(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same product relation $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. To isolate the system factor, divide by $X(s)$ and keep the ratio order $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the given transforms. The new wrinkle is the shifted factor $(s-3)$ in both terms, but the cancellation pattern is the same because the shared pole factor appears in numerator and denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{\\frac{8}{s-3}}{\\frac{2}{s-3}}=\\frac{8}{s-3}\\cdot\\frac{s-3}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After cancellation, $H(s)=4$. The transfer function is still the system-only multiplier, and the shifted denominator does not change the ratio when the shared factor matches.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $H(s)$ as the System-Only Multiplier",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}$ and $Y(s)=\\frac{8}{s+1}$, find $H(s)$ and explain why the ratio represents only the system, not the specific input.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is still the product relation $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. To isolate the system factor, divide by $X(s)$ and keep the ratio order $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the given transforms. Both $X(s)$ and $Y(s)$ share the same factor $(s+1)$, so the algebra should cancel that input-dependent part and leave only the system ratio.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}=\\frac{\\frac{8}{s+1}}{\\frac{2}{s+1}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After cancellation, $H(s)=4$. That result is the system-only multiplier: it describes the LTI system itself, while the specific input only changes $X(s)$ and therefore $Y(s)$ through the same ratio.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing the Same Transfer Function Across Different Inputs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Experiment A: $X_1(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}$, $Y_1(s)=\\frac{3}{s+2}$. Experiment B: $X_2(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}$, $Y_2(s)=\\frac{15}{s+2}$. Compute the transfer function from each and verify whether they match.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same product relation in each experiment: $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. To identify the system-only factor, divide each output transform by its matching input transform and keep the ratio order $H(s)=Y(s)/X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H_1(s)=\\frac{Y_1(s)}{X_1(s)},\\qquad H_2(s)=\\frac{Y_2(s)}{X_2(s)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the two pairs. Each experiment has the same shared factor $(s+2)$, so the cancellation pattern is identical even though the amplitudes are different.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H_1(s)=\\frac{\\frac{3}{s+2}}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}=3,\\qquad H_2(s)=\\frac{\\frac{15}{s+2}}{\\frac{5}{s+2}}=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both ratios give $H(s)=3$, so the transfer function is the same across the two input-output pairs. That matching ratio is the system characteristic, while the specific input only changes the numerical scale of $X(s)$ and $Y(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
